      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T12:29:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could you document what this task is trying to check? It seems if a blocked task has woken up on na CPU it shouldn\u0027t be running on?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3795,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 3795,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "498de7c6984ee0a65d5923f4ee4bea20dc7353fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0caa1d8a_28e7fe7f",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 3795,
      "author": {
        "id": 1945845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T23:16:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, basically if the blocked task has been migrated, we want to migrate it back before running it. I\u0027ve add some comments to v15 to explain this better.",
      "parentUuid": "b5096248_8a07854e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3795,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 3795,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "498de7c6984ee0a65d5923f4ee4bea20dc7353fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8f4c69ef_eb4b6045",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 6887,
      "author": {
        "id": 1976445
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T12:29:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "load_balance() tends to hold the two rqs when it migrates tasks. I\u0027m not sure if we can end up with races with load_balance() tries to do something with this task too.\n\nI assume this path can never trigger for a running task right? You intercept the wake up path to force the migration before we enqueue it. Just double checking.\n\nWhat about migrate_disable() sections? Seems out of the scope of this patch, but beside affinity, a task might need to stay on an CPU because it is on migrate_disable() section so we need to ensure it moves back to that CPU when it wakes up.\n\n(sorry have\u0027t looked at later patches yet if this is handled later)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6886,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6887,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "498de7c6984ee0a65d5923f4ee4bea20dc7353fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a8f8e46e_61657087",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 6887,
      "author": {
        "id": 1945845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T23:16:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I have run with an mdelay(1) right here between the unlock and lock calls to try to tease out any potential races. So far I\u0027ve not hit anything.\n\nBut I\u0027ll need to look a bit more to see how load_balance might interact. \n\nWe never proxy_migrate_task() on a running task. Its always migrating from the current rq to the target, and we do the curr_in_chain check in find_proxy_task() in order to avoid trying to migrate current (when we do have to proxy_migrate current, we first proxy_resched_idle(), switch to idle\u0027s context, and come back in to migrate the blocked task chain.\n\nAnd yeah, the proxy_needs_return() and the task-\u003eblocked_on_state management makes sure we always return-migrate a proxy-migrated task before we run it.\n\nRegarding migrate_disable() sections, can you clarify what you\u0027re thinking of? I want to make sure I\u0027m not missing something. We do allow the task to be proxy-migrated as needed, but it will be BO_BLOCKED or BO_WAKING, not BO_RUNNABLE so it won\u0027t run, but it may be moved between runqueues in that blocked state. I want to make sure there\u0027s not some other requirement of migrate_disable() that I\u0027m missing that wants to preserve the task on the rq for some other reason.",
      "parentUuid": "8f4c69ef_eb4b6045",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6886,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6887,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "498de7c6984ee0a65d5923f4ee4bea20dc7353fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf6b2cda_06b5a838",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 6887,
      "author": {
        "id": 1976445
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-11T13:40:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "IIRC load_balance() tends to hold the src and target rq in specific order before doing the migration. And I\u0027m not sure, but maybe we need to do the same here.\n\n\u003e Regarding migrate_disable() sections, can you clarify what you\u0027re thinking of?\n\nI think this is unlikely a scenario we hit outside PREEMPT_RT as I don\u0027t recall seeing many users for preempt_disable(). But one of the blocked tasks could be in preempt_disabled() section. And similar to affinity where the chain can migrate to a CPU where it is not supposed to run on, we might need to ensure it moves back to the CPU it was supposed to run on when it wakes up.\n\nI think preempt_disable() is not something we likely hit in Android though.",
      "parentUuid": "a8f8e46e_61657087",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6886,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6887,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "498de7c6984ee0a65d5923f4ee4bea20dc7353fe",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68265cc2_d086e2f6",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 6887,
      "author": {
        "id": 1945845
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-12-12T05:56:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So yeah, I think you\u0027re talking about double_lock_balance() which grabs both rqs together (ordering by address, I believe so they avoid ABBA). I think that makes sense when you don\u0027t hold a rq lock and need to aquire both.\n\nBut in our case, we are already holding the current rq lock. So deactivating the task, dropping the rq lock, taking the target_rq lock, activating the task and dropping the target_rq lock seems reasonable here. \n\nThere\u0027s a number of places where the rq locks are taken and released separately in a similar fashion.\n\nActually, one example is active_load_balance_cpu_stop() [https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/kernel/sched/fair.c#n11987]is one of those. \n\nIt locks the busiest rq first:[https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/kernel/sched/fair.c#n11997], \n\ndeactivates the task:[https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/kernel/sched/fair.c#n12043] \n\nReleases the busiest rq lock:\n[https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/kernel/sched/fair.c#n12055],\n\nand then calls attach_one_task with the dst rq:\n[https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/kernel/sched/fair.c#n12058]\n\nwhich locks it and activates the task on the dst rq:\n[https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/kernel/sched/fair.c#n9649]\n\nThe gap you highlight here where we don\u0027t hold either rq and have a deactivated task does look concerning at first, but when the task is deactivated, it calls `WRITE_ONCE(p-\u003eon_rq, TASK_ON_RQ_MIGRATING);` before dequeuing, so the task is marked as in-process of migrating, so other CPUs won\u0027t try to manipulate it.\n\nWrt preempt_disable(), I\u0027m still not sure I understand your point.  My sense is that preempt_disable() is what prevents the current running task from being preempted. So things like the CPU stopper can\u0027t jump in to get that task switched off the CPU until preempt_enable is called. preempt_disable() effectively keeps current on the CPU, so its not a migratable task.\n\nBut again, please do clarify your concerns, as I want to make sure I\u0027m not misunderstanding you or somehow missing something here.",
      "parentUuid": "bf6b2cda_06b5a838",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 6886,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 6887,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "498de7c6984ee0a65d5923f4ee4bea20dc7353fe",
